{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NoncommRing","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower"],"declarations":21,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.framing_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.framing_factor","kind":"theorem","line":459,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L459-L464","doc":"**The framing factor has no square roots.** `[(A/q)·(−A³)⁻¹]^e = q^{-2e}`,\nwritten with `a = A² = −q` so that only even powers of `A` appear:\n`(A/q)·(−A³)⁻¹ = −1/(q·A²) = −1/(q·(−q)) = q⁻²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.kauffman_loop","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.kauffman_loop","kind":"theorem","line":447,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L447-L457","doc":"**The Kauffman dictionary.** With `a := A² = −q`, the bracket loop value\n`−A² − A⁻²` is `[2]_q`.\n\nHence `t = A⁻⁴ = q⁻²`. Note `A` itself is a square root of `−q` and does *not*\nappear in the final normalisation: the odd powers of `A` in `ρ(σᵢ) = (A/q)σᵢ` and\nin the writhe factor `(−A³)^{-e}` cancel, leaving `q^{-2e}`. The half-argument\nquantity `q^{1/2} + q^{-1/2}` never enters. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.interface_factorisation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.interface_factorisation","kind":"theorem","line":423,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L423-L439","doc":"**The interface factorisation.** `p₊` positive crossings contribute `z` each,\n`p₋` negative ones contribute `w = z − h` each, multiplicatively.\n\nStated with the crossings already gathered at the peelable end, because that is\nwhat the Markov axiom licenses: `tr(y·σₙ) = z·tr(y)` needs `σₙ` to be the *top*\ngenerator, outside the subalgebra `y` lives in. That the interface crossings of a\nnuclear braid can be brought into that position is the physical input of\n`prf:interface-knot-markov-z` — a claim about `atom_braid_word_3A`, not about the\ntrace — and is **not** proved here. What is proved is that once they are, the\nfactorisation is exact, and exact for *every* Markov parameter. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.peel_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.peel_pow","kind":"theorem","line":411,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L411-L421","doc":"**Iterated Markov peel.** One peel hypothesis, applied `k` times. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.conventionD_iff_markov_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.conventionD_iff_markov_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":375,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L375-L390","doc":"**The characterisation.** Bringing the two together: the normalisation\nsurvives both stabilisation moves **iff** the Markov parameter is convention D.\n\nWith `jonesV_conj` and Markov's theorem this says convention D is the *unique*\nMarkov parameter for which `q^{-2e}[2]_q^{n-1}tr_M` is a link invariant. The\nuniqueness is the part that matters for the excision: a wrong loop parameter does\nnot give a slightly-off invariant, it gives something that is not an invariant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.jonesV_conj","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.jonesV_conj","kind":"theorem","line":365,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L365-L373","doc":"**Conjugation invariance.** `e` and `m` are unchanged by conjugation and the\ntrace is cyclic, so nothing needs proving beyond transporting the trace equality.\nRecorded because Markov's theorem needs *both* moves, and it costs one line —\nleaving it implicit would make the invariance argument look narrower than it is.\n\nHolds for any Markov parameter; it is the stabilisation moves, not this one, that\npin convention D. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.jonesV_stabilise_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.jonesV_stabilise_neg","kind":"theorem","line":342,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L342-L363","doc":"**`jonesV` is invariant under negative stabilisation.** `e ↦ e−1`,\n`m ↦ m+1`, `tr ↦ (z−h)·tr`. Stated with the source exponent as `e + 1` so that\nthe target is `e` — no truncated subtraction anywhere. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.jonesV_stabilise_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.jonesV_stabilise_pos","kind":"theorem","line":330,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L330-L340","doc":"**`jonesV` is invariant under positive stabilisation.** `e ↦ e+1`,\n`m ↦ m+1`, `tr ↦ z·tr`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.stab_neg_iff_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.stab_neg_iff_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":311,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L311-L328","doc":"**Negative stabilisation ⟺ convention D.** The move\n`β ↦ βσ_n⁻¹` sends `e ↦ e−1`, `m ↦ m+1`, and by `sigma_inv` multiplies the trace\nby `z − h`. Invariance is `q²·[2]_q·(z−h) = 1`, again equivalent to convention D.\n\nThat the two stabilisations force the *same* `z` is not automatic — it is a\nconsistency check on the whole normalisation, and it passes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.stab_pos_iff_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.stab_pos_iff_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":295,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L295-L309","doc":"**Positive stabilisation ⟺ convention D.** The move\n`β ∈ B_n ↦ βσ_n ∈ B_{n+1}` sends `e ↦ e+1`, `m ↦ m+1` and multiplies the trace\nby `z`. Invariance is the single identity `q⁻²·[2]_q·z = 1` — and that identity\nis equivalent to `z(q²+1) = q³`.\n\nOnly `q ≠ 0` is needed in either direction; no `q² + 1 ≠ 0` side condition,\nbecause if `q² + 1 = 0` then `[2]_q = 0` and both sides are false. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.two_component_unlink","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.two_component_unlink","kind":"theorem","line":264,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L264-L280","doc":"**The two-component unlink.** Closure of `σ₁σ₃ ∈ B₄`: `e = 2`, `n = 4`,\n`tr_M(σ₁σ₃) = z²`.\n\n`q⁻⁴ · [2]_q³ · z² = [2]_q = q + q⁻¹`, which is `−t^{1/2} − t^{-1/2}` — the Jones\npolynomial of the 2-component unlink.\n\nThe sharpest of the four checks. It has the *same* trace value `z²` as\n`unknot_three_strand` and a different answer, purely through `n`. Any account\nthat reads a Jones polynomial off a raw trace value — as `markov-pair-z2` does —\ncannot distinguish these two links at all. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.hopf_link","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.hopf_link","kind":"theorem","line":249,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L249-L262","doc":"**The Hopf link.** Closure of `σ₁² ∈ B₂`: `e = 2`, `n = 2`.\n\n`q⁻⁴ · [2]_q · (hz + 1) = q⁻¹ + q⁻⁵`.\n\nIn the Jones variable `t = q⁻²` with `t^{1/2} := A⁻² = −q⁻¹`, this is\n`−t^{1/2} − t^{5/2}` — the standard value for the positive Hopf link. The sign\nlives entirely in the branch of `t^{1/2}`; in `q` there is no ambiguity, which is\none practical reason to state the normalisation in `q` rather than `t`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.trace_sigma_sq_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.trace_sigma_sq_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":237,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L237-L247","doc":"**The Markov trace of `σ₁²`.** `tr_M(σ₁²) = hz + 1` with `h = q − q⁻¹`, from\nthe quadratic relation and the Markov peel. Under convention D this is\n`(q⁴+1)/(q²+1)`.\n\nThis — not `z²` — is the Hopf-link trace. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.unknot_three_strand","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.unknot_three_strand","kind":"theorem","line":222,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L222-L235","doc":"**Unknot again, as the closure of `σ₁σ₂ ∈ B₃`.** `e = 2`, `n = 3`,\n`tr_M(σ₁σ₂) = z²`.\n\n`q⁻⁴ · [2]_q² · z² = 1`. This is the closure `markov-pair-z2` calls the Hopf\nlink. It is the **unknot** — and the formula says so, independently of the\n`σ₁ ∈ B₂` computation above, which is a real consistency check on the `n`- and\n`e`-dependence rather than a restatement. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.unknot_two_strand","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.unknot_two_strand","kind":"theorem","line":212,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L212-L220","doc":"**Unknot as the closure of `σ₁ ∈ B₂`.** `e = 1`, `n = 2`, `tr_M(σ₁) = z`.\n\n`q⁻² · [2]_q · z = 1`. The base case that fixes the normalisation: any other\nchoice of framing exponent or loop power fails here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.z_eq_div","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.z_eq_div","kind":"theorem","line":207,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L207-L210","doc":"Convention D in solved form: `z = q³/(q²+1)`. Convenient for substitution. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.q_sq_add_one_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.q_sq_add_one_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L195-L205","doc":"**`q² + 1 ≠ 0` is forced, not assumed.** In a general field it could vanish\n(characteristic `2`, or `q = i` over `ℂ`), but convention D rules it out: if\n`q² + 1 = 0` then `q³ = z(q²+1) = 0`, so `q = 0`. Needed to clear denominators in\nevery identity below, and worth isolating so the hypothesis list stays honest —\nnone of these theorems needs an ad-hoc side condition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.jonesV","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.jonesV","kind":"def","line":191,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L191-L193","doc":"The normalised invariant `V = q^{-2e}·[2]_q^{m}·tr`, with `tr` the Markov\ntrace of the braid and `m = n − 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.jonesFactor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.jonesFactor","kind":"def","line":181,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L181-L189","doc":"The Jones normalisation factor `q^{-2e} · [2]_q^{m}`, where **`m = n − 1`**\nis the strand count less one.\n\nCarrying `m` rather than `n` is deliberate: `n - 1` in `ℕ` is truncated\nsubtraction, which would silently do the wrong thing at `n = 0` and force a side\ncondition on every lemma. With `m` the stabilisation moves are `m ↦ m + 1` and\nnothing is ever subtracted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.sigma_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.sigma_inv","kind":"theorem","line":153,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L153-L166","doc":"**The inverse braid generator is `σ − h`.** From the quadratic relation\nalone: `σ(σ − h) = σ² − σh = (hσ + 1) − hσ = 1`, and symmetrically on the left.\n\nThis is what makes negative stabilisation computable without ever inverting\nanything: `tr_M(σ⁻¹) = z − h`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.tl_generator_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.JonesNormalisation.tl_generator_sq","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/JonesNormalisation.lean#L120-L151","doc":"**`eᵢ := q − σᵢ` is a Temperley–Lieb generator with loop parameter `[2]_q`.**\n\nFrom the Hecke quadratic relation `σ² = (q − q⁻¹)σ + 1` alone — no trace, no\nconvention. This is what makes `[2]_q` the loop value and hence what makes\n`q^{1/2} + q^{-1/2}` the wrong prefactor in `markov-pair-z2`.\n\nStated in an arbitrary `R`-algebra rather than a commutative ring: `σᵢ` does not\ncommute with `σⱼ`, and the point is that this identity needs no such assumption,\nonly that scalars are central (`Algebra.commutes`). ","signatureChunk":0}]}